# Chroma library, modern Earth: CLASH MOMENTS (v25 prep, Library, 10-23, round 2). Goes to chroma-library/earth_clash.lib.
# Syntax: header of build.py, plus the v25 keys clash:, band: (moment) and clash: (option), and the slots
# {clash.setting}, {clash.holder}, {clash.demand}, {clash.ally}, {clash.scene} (POUR-NOTES.md).
# Rule: chroma-ideas/life-drivers-2026-10-20/v25-specs.md, card 3; keys: chroma-engine/notes/v25/other-shapes.md, 3
# (frozen contract: seven settings, {clash.demand}, give_in read three ways by the engine).
# Fourteen templates: seven settings (household, school, work, congregation, town, group, land) x two bands.
# per_year is 0 everywhere: the Engine sends a clash when it applies.
# Balance layout: five one-colour options and one full cross cycle (s1 W>U U>B B>R R>G G>W, s2 W>B U>R B>G R>W G>U),
# seven templates on each cycle. Each kind has one single and one cross.
# The demand itself comes from the Engine's pressure record through {clash.demand}, which every scene carries after a
# colon. The rest of the scene gives one concrete, neutral detail and the stakes, and never names the ways; options are
# worded to fit any demand the setting can make.
# give_in: the Engine reads each one as willing agreement, strategic accommodation or constrained obedience from the
# life's conviction and dependence, so both give_in labels and footprints are worded to read right under all three
# (what is done, never why or how it feels).
# A dependent child has no exit from the household: the teen household leaves are a withdrawing into their own time.
# Other teen leaves are bounded and safe (a grandparent's for the summer, another school through proper channels,
# a year's wait, quitting a Saturday job or a club), and may only half work.
# Land clashes are about ecological stakes and people's commitments; the land takes no side.
# Nothing here is built or run by this writer.
#
# Plan: which colour takes which kind (single colour + cross), per template.
#
#   template              cycle  confront   give_in    leave      own_scene  bridge
#   household teen        s1     W + R>G    U + G>W    B + W>U    R + U>B    G + B>R
#   household adult       s2     G + B>G    W + R>W    U + G>U    B + W>B    R + U>R
#   school teen           s2     R + U>R    G + B>G    W + R>W    U + G>U    B + W>B
#   school adult          s1     B + W>U    R + U>B    G + B>R    W + R>G    U + G>W
#   work teen             s1     U + G>W    B + W>U    R + U>B    G + B>R    W + R>G
#   work adult            s2     W + R>W    U + G>U    B + W>B    R + U>R    G + B>G
#   congregation teen     s2     G + B>G    W + R>W    U + G>U    B + W>B    R + U>R
#   congregation adult    s1     R + U>B    G + B>R    W + R>G    U + G>W    B + W>U
#   town teen             s1     B + W>U    R + U>B    G + B>R    W + R>G    U + G>W
#   town adult            s2     U + G>U    B + W>B    R + U>R    G + B>G    W + R>W
#   group teen            s2     W + R>W    U + G>U    B + W>B    R + U>R    G + B>G
#   group adult           s1     G + B>R    W + R>G    U + G>W    B + W>U    R + U>B
#   land teen             s1     R + U>B    G + B>R    W + R>G    U + G>W    B + W>U
#   land adult            s2     B + W>B    R + U>R    G + B>G    W + R>W    U + G>U
#
# Colour by kind over the fourteen templates: singles + crosses led = total (each cell's ideal is 5.6).
#
#        confront    give_in     leave       own_scene   bridge
#   W    3 + 3 = 6   3 + 2 = 5   3 + 3 = 6   3 + 3 = 6   2 + 3 = 5
#   U    2 + 3 = 5   3 + 3 = 6   3 + 2 = 5   3 + 3 = 6   3 + 3 = 6
#   B    3 + 3 = 6   2 + 3 = 5   3 + 3 = 6   3 + 2 = 5   3 + 3 = 6
#   R    3 + 3 = 6   3 + 3 = 6   2 + 3 = 5   3 + 3 = 6   3 + 2 = 5
#   G    3 + 2 = 5   3 + 3 = 6   3 + 3 = 6   2 + 3 = 5   3 + 3 = 6
#
# As the second colour of a cross: W 3 3 2 3 3, U 3 3 3 2 3, B 3 3 3 3 2, R 2 3 3 3 3, G 3 2 3 3 3 (same column order).

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- household, teen (cycle s1)

== the new rule at home
tier: life event
clash: household
band: teen
stages: juvenile young_adult
age: 10-19
per_year: 0 0 0 0 0 0
tone: trouble
life: family, home
horizon: months
roles: parent, sibling, friend
earth: a new rule at home, laid down without discussion, and it lands on what matters most to {N}
tribal: a new rule of the hearth, said once at the fire, and it lands on what matters most to {N}
magic: a new rule of the house, written into its wards, and it lands on what matters most to {N}
scene: A new rule has come down from {clash.holder} for everyone in {clash.setting}: {clash.demand}. It was said once at dinner, and it is not up for discussion. It lands squarely on the thing that matters most to {N} this year, and the whole house is waiting to see what {N} does.
scene tribal: The word from {clash.holder} has been said at the fire: {clash.demand}. No exceptions. It lands on the very thing {N} cares about most, and the whole hearth is waiting to see what {N} does.
scene magic: On the word of {clash.holder}, the rule of {clash.setting} is now this: {clash.demand}. It is written into the house's wards. It lands on the one thing {N} lives for, and the household is waiting to see what {N} does.
- W | write down why the rule is unfair, and ask for a proper family vote | v: universalism, conformity | clash: confront | foot: lesson rules can be argued fairly | chance: 50
- U | keep to the rule until the end of term, noting how it goes | v: self-direction, security | clash: give_in | foot: means freedom-.03 | chance: 85
- B | keep to your own room in the evenings, on your own projects | v: power, self-direction | clash: leave | foot: means ties-.03 | chance: 70
- R | spend every free hour you do have with the crowd at {clash.scene} | v: stimulation, hedonism | clash: own_scene | foot: place gather.circle; person friend | chance: 75
- G | ask {clash.ally} to sit down one evening with them and {clash.holder} | v: benevolence, tradition | clash: bridge | foot: means ties+.03 | chance: 60
- W>U | set a strict timetable of your own, and spend the evenings by it in your room | v: conformity, self-direction | clash: leave | foot: means time+.03 | chance: 75
- U>B | find a club of your own at school, and soon help run it | v: self-direction, power | clash: own_scene | foot: place gather.hall | chance: 70
- B>R | offer a trade: keep to it most of the week, with one evening free of it | v: power, stimulation | clash: bridge | foot: lesson a fair trade can settle things | chance: 55
- R>G | argue it out loud at dinner, with {sibling} on your side | v: stimulation, benevolence | clash: confront | foot: mark defied an authority | chance: 45
- G>W | agree to the rule, and keep to it at home | v: benevolence, conformity | clash: give_in | foot: mark kept your word | chance: 90
works: The rule stays, with one exception carved out for what matters most to {N}. Nobody quite won, and the house is calmer for it.
works: It takes a month of trying, and by the end of term the rule has softened, on conditions {N} did not choose.
fails: Nothing moves. The rule stands as it was, and the house goes quiet in a cold way.
fails: It half works: the rule bends for two weeks, then snaps back the first time {N} slips.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- household, adult (cycle s2)

== the list on the kitchen wall
tier: life event
clash: household
band: adult
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 18-90
per_year: 0 0 0 0 0 0
tone: trouble
life: family, home, money
horizon: months
roles: parent, partner, sibling, friend
earth: a list on the kitchen wall says how the house is run now, and one line of it cuts across {Ns} plans
tribal: the elders of the hearth have shared out the winter's work and stores, and one share costs {N} dearly
magic: a sealed scroll of household rules hangs by the hearth, and one rule cuts across {Ns} plans
scene: A list is pinned to the kitchen wall, in the hand of {clash.holder}: how {clash.setting} will be run from now on. Most of it is fair. One line is not: {clash.demand}. It cuts straight across the life {N} has been building.
scene tribal: The elders of the hearth have shared out the winter's work and stores. Most of it is fair. The part that falls on {N} is not: {clash.demand}. It takes what {N} had set aside for something of {Ns} own, and {clash.holder} will not hear a word against it.
scene magic: A new scroll of household rules hangs by the hearth, sealed by {clash.holder}. Most of it is fair. One rule is not: {clash.demand}. It takes the very hours {N} had planned for.
- W | agree to the list as written, and keep your part of it | v: conformity, security | clash: give_in | foot: mark kept your word | chance: 90
- U | move out to a place of your own, with the sums done first | v: self-direction, achievement | clash: leave | foot: mark left home | chance: 65
- B | spend your evenings with the people at {clash.scene}, who take your plans seriously | v: power, achievement | clash: own_scene | foot: place gather.circle | chance: 75
- R | cook a meal for the whole house, and say warmly what you need | v: stimulation, benevolence | clash: bridge | foot: means ties+.03 | chance: 60
- G | remind {clash.holder} how this family always did it, and that it worked | v: tradition, benevolence | clash: confront | foot: lesson old ways can argue too | chance: 50
- W>B | join the residents' committee, where your way of running things is welcome | v: conformity, power | clash: own_scene | foot: place rule.counsel | chance: 70
- U>R | write a new list with {clash.ally}, and bring it to the house together | v: self-direction, stimulation | clash: bridge | foot: person go-between | chance: 55
- B>G | say plainly where the line is, and that it holds unless something changes | v: power, security | clash: confront | foot: person rival | chance: 45
- R>W | keep to the new line as written, and let the rest of the list be | v: security, conformity | clash: give_in | foot: means freedom-.03 | chance: 90
- G>U | move back to the town you grew up in, and think it through there | v: tradition, self-direction | clash: leave | foot: mark moved away | chance: 60
works: The line is rewritten, not the way {N} wanted it, but in a way {N} can live with. The list stays on the wall.
works: {N} gets most of it back, and pays for it with a bigger share of the chores.
fails: Nothing changes. The list stays as it is, and the kitchen is a tense place for a while.
fails: It half works: half the line is settled, and the rest is argued over every week.

